From faa795461e0a32e0bea687e317d303846eadb943 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gaia <build@heygaia.io>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 01:41:58 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] fix(cdp): Page.navigate answers when the document commits,
 and reports each lifecycle step as it happens

Page.navigate answered only after the whole load (stylesheets, every script,
frames) or the end-to-end OBSCURA_NAV_TIMEOUT_MS deadline, and then emitted
init, commit, DOMContentLoaded, load and networkIdle together with one
timestamp. A client with Chrome's expectations (answer at commit, then wait
for lifecycle events) timed the call out on slow pages, and the deadline left
pages whose scripts outran it without them.

The page now reports its navigation steps as they happen
(NavigationProgress: Committed, DomContentLoaded, Load). The server answers
Page.navigate and Page.reload at the commit with frameId and loaderId, emits
the document's request and frameNavigated then, DOMContentLoaded and load
when the document fires them, and the remaining requests, networkIdle (only
when nothing is in flight) and frameStoppedLoading when loading ends. A script
that navigates again while loading commits under a new loader. A navigation
that fails before committing answers with errorText, as Chrome does, not a
protocol error. History traversal and script-started navigations report
through the same events.

OBSCURA_NAV_TIMEOUT_MS now bounds getting the document, up to commit; what the
committed document loads is bounded by each phase's own budget.

Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Tm2SNEJ42hMPiZ6cFb8Vih
